00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
an illegal road cuts through what used  to be pristine rainforest  around it a cemetery of centuries old  trees reduced to dust  look those are big patches and most  likely for cocoa fields or cattle  ranching which will definitely  disconnect this area  rodrigo botero runs an ngo that protects  the colombian amazon he brought us on a  five-hour long flight across five  national parks and supposedly protected  indigenous lands  none are intact criminal hands are  grabbing land and burning trees for  intensive agriculture cattle ranching or  coca farming  the magnitude and the intensity of the  destruction that we are able to see over  five national parks in the most  biodiverse area of colombia in the  middle of the fragile transition between  the andes and the amazon is a warning  call not only for colombia but for  humanity as a whole the colombian amazon  has lost over a million hectares of  rainforest in the last five years an  area larger than the island of cyprus  rodrigo says a complex web of interests  are to blame  first of all this has to do with the  international conglomerates interested  in cheap land for the world's  agro-commodities market  more than a million heads of cattle have  been introduced in this area surrounding  the chiribiquete national park alone it  is also a way to launder money from drug  trafficking and illegal mining  the level of deforestation accelerated  after the government signed a peace deal  with farc rebels in 2006.  today huge branches extend for as far as  the eye can see one of the first things  you notice as you walk through these  burnt patches of the jungle is the  silence gone are the sounds of the  animals the birds the insects that live  in the forest all that amazing  biodiversity  turned into ashes  colombian president ivan duque pledged  to cut deforestation by half by the end  of his term in august an overtly  optimistic promise  yet a military anti-deforestation  initiative and new laws targeting those  causing environmental devastation are  starting to pay dividends  the first years of this operation have  been questionable because the military  exclusively went after the poor people  logging and not the masterminds we also  had an outdated legal framework because  believe it or not grabbing land wasn't a  crime here but under pressure  internationally things have started to  change in recent months a major land  grabber and two local mayors were  arrested  changes that offer a glimmer of hope  that things could improve before it's  too late for all alessandra ampietti al  jazeera san jose
 

